omarchy-theme-set copies the official theme with `cp -r`, which keeps the
source's modes. On Arch those files are writable; on Guix they come from
the read-only store, so the staged theme's backgrounds/ directory was
read-only. The next theme change then could not remove the current theme:
`rm -rf` failed inside backgrounds/, and `mv` moved the new theme into the
old directory instead of replacing it. current/theme was left without
foot.ini and the other generated configs, and foot reported
"current/theme/foot.ini: no such file or directory". Make the staged copy
the owner's, and make a current theme staged before this fix removable,
so the next theme change repairs it (test_omarchy_theme_set.py). Roguix
only: remove once Omarchy stages the theme without the source's modes.

--- a/bin/omarchy-theme-set
+++ b/bin/omarchy-theme-set
@@ -267,6 +267,7 @@
 
 # Copy official theme first, then overlay the user's theme on top
 cp -r "$OMARCHY_THEMES_PATH/$THEME_NAME/"* "$NEXT_THEME_PATH/" 2>/dev/null
+chmod -R u+w "$NEXT_THEME_PATH"
 
 if theme_came_from_a_repo "$USER_THEMES_PATH/$THEME_NAME"; then
   stage_installed_theme "$USER_THEMES_PATH/$THEME_NAME"
@@ -289,6 +290,7 @@
 fi
 
 # Swap next theme in as current
+chmod -R u+w "$CURRENT_THEME_PATH" 2>/dev/null
 rm -rf "$CURRENT_THEME_PATH"
 mv "$NEXT_THEME_PATH" "$CURRENT_THEME_PATH"
 
